>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `register_typedef`

``` hol4
Refute.register_typedef :
  {ty : hol_type,
   abs : term,
   rep : term,
   absrep_thms : thm list} -> unit
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Registers an existing type definition with the model finder.

`ty` is the abstract type.  `abs` and `rep` must be existing constants
with mutually opposite abstract and representation types.  `absrep_thms`
is either a singleton list holding the standard, hypothesis-free
conjunction of the Abs/Rep bijection properties exported for the type, or
a two-element list holding its two conjuncts as separate theorems, in
either order: `!a. abs (rep a) = a` and `!r. P r = (rep (abs r) = r)`.
Refute recovers its representation predicate and verifies that predicate
against the type's `<tyop>_TY_DEF` theorem; a two-theorem `absrep_thms`
is combined into the same conjunction first, so the two forms register
identically.

Type-operator parameters must be distinct type variables and the
representation type may not introduce extra type variables.
Registrations are session-local; a valid later registration replaces the
same typedef type operator.  The model finder also lazily harvests
standard typedef metadata when possible.

### Failure

Fails for malformed type parameters, missing or mismatched type-definition
metadata, nonconstant or ill-typed Abs/Rep terms, theorem hypotheses or
an unsupported theorem shape, an `absrep_thms` list that is neither one
nor two theorems long, two theorems that are the same bijection half, two
theorems naming different Abs/Rep constants, or an incompatible datatype,
codatatype, quotient, or fraction classification.

### See also

[`Refute.register_quotient`](#Refute.register_quotient),
[`Refute.register_codatatype`](#Refute.register_codatatype),
[`Refute.register_frac_type`](#Refute.register_frac_type),
[`Refute.harvest_registrations`](#Refute.harvest_registrations),
[`Refute.model_refute`](#Refute.model_refute)

Typedef encoding is described in \ref{Description:the-holrefute-library}.
